<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gurzil</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gurzil was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> god worshipped by the Laguatan and other tribes of Tripolitania in late antiquity, represented as a bull and said to be the son of the god Ammon and a cow. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corippus">Corippus</a> describes his image being carried into battle against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> in the 540s by the chief Ierna, who was also his priest. He is one of the few Berber gods named in ancient sources.</p></div>
